-- title:
--   A maximal ideal of the k₀-chart contracting to y₁
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime and $M'$ a nonzero natural number with $q \nmid M'$, and $\ell_g$ is a prime with $\ell_g \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ which divides $M'$ and is listed among the prime factors of $M'$ (`hℓgM'` and `hℓgpf`).
--
--   First group (the valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and its constant reduction). $A$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` satisfying `A.LiesOverPrime q`, that is, $q$ is a non-unit of $A$. $W$ is a finite set of places of `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'` — the intermediate field of `LaurentSeries (ResidueField A)` generated over the residue field of $A$ by the two series `jqModC` and `jqNModC … M'` — over that residue field, and `hW` says that the members of $W$ are exactly the places belonging to `ssPlaces q M' (ResidueField A)`, i.e. the places satisfying the predicate `IsSupersingularPlace q M'`. The hypothesis `hle` asserts that `modularFunctionFieldBar M'`, the subfield of `LaurentSeries (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)` generated over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ by the coefficientwise images of the full level-$M'$ modular function field over $\mathbb{Q}$, is contained in `fieldBar q M' = xHFunctionFieldBar (q ^ 2 * M') (levelH q M')`. Next, $R_0$ is a `ConstantReduction` datum for $A$ with upper field `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` and residual field `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'`: it consists of a valuation subring `R₀.integers` of the upper field, a surjective ring map `R₀.residue` onto the residual field whose kernel is the maximal ideal of `R₀.integers`, and a map `R₀.placeMap` on places, subject to the clauses of that structure (an element of $A$ is characterised by its image lying in `R₀.integers`; `R₀.residue` is compatible with the residue map of $A$; every nonzero element of the upper field can be rescaled by a constant so as to have nonzero residue; `R₀.placeMap` preserves degrees and transports principal divisors). The hypothesis `hR₀` requires that for every Laurent series $y$ with coefficients in $A$ whose image under `coeffMap A.subtype` lies in `modularFunctionFieldBar M'`, that image lies in `R₀.integers` and its $R_0$-residue, read as an element of `LaurentSeries (ResidueField A)`, is the coefficientwise reduction `coeffMap (IsLocalRing.residue ↥A) y`.
--
--   Second group (the base field $k_0$ and its local ring). $k_0$ is an intermediate field of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q}$ and $\pi_0 \in k_0$ has image in $A$ (`hπ`). Write $A_0$ for `A.comap (algebraMap ↥k₀ (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))`, the valuation subring of $k_0$ obtained by pulling $A$ back along $k_0 \hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. The hypotheses on $A_0$ are: `hdvr`, that $A_0$ is a discrete valuation ring; `hunif`, that its maximal ideal is generated by $\pi_0$; `hhens`, that $A_0$ is a Henselian local ring; `hres`, that the residue field of $A_0$ is algebraically closed; and `hκ`, that every $a \in A$ is congruent modulo the maximal ideal of $A$ to some element $c$ of $k_0$ lying in $A$. Further, $\xi \in k_0$ is a primitive $q$-th root of unity (`hξ`), and `hιξ` provides a ring homomorphism $k_0 \to \mathbb{C}$ sending $\xi$ to $\exp(2\pi i/q)$.
--
--   Third group (level and the modular function field over $k_0$). $H_1$ is a subgroup of $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times$, and `hH₁` identifies it as the intersection of `levelH q M'`, the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/q)^\times$, with the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/\ell_g)^\times$; thus $H_1$ consists of the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q$ and modulo $\ell_g$. $K$ is an intermediate field of `LaurentSeries ↥k₀` over $k_0$, and `hK` identifies it with `laurentBaseChange ↥k₀ (xHFunctionField (q ^ 2 * M') H₁)`, the subfield generated over $k_0$ by the coefficientwise images of the $\mathbb{Q}$-rational function field of level $q^2M'$ and subgroup $H_1$; $K$ carries an $A_0$-algebra structure compatible with $A_0 \to k_0 \to K$. Finally $j \in K$ is a nonzero element whose underlying Laurent series is [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb ↥k₀ ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81), the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant $j$ pushed from $\mathbb{Q}$ to $k_0$.
--
--   Fourth group (Weierstrass and moduli bookkeeping over $A_0$, stated for all $A_0$-algebras $T$). `hℓ`: for every commutative $A_0$-algebra $T$, Weierstrass curve $W$ over $T$, variable change $C$ and level datum $D$ (a quadruple $x_P, y_P, x_Q, y_Q \in T$), if `IsGamma1Point W ℓg D` holds — $(x_P,y_P)$ satisfies the affine equation of $W$, the division polynomial `W.preΨ ℓg` vanishes at $x_P$, and $x_Q = x_P$, $y_Q = y_P$ — then the same holds for $C \bullet W$ and the transformed datum `D.variableChange C`. `hM`: for every such $T$, $W$, $C$, naturals $p,k$ and $h \in T[X]$, `IsGamma0PowAt W p k h` implies `IsGamma0PowAt (C • W) p k (kernelVariableChangeDeg C (gamma0PowDeg p k) h)`, where `IsGamma0PowAt` is the two-kernel condition when $p^k = 2$ and the cyclic generator kernel condition otherwise. Next, $\mathcal{G}$ is a family of relative group laws `GroupLaws` on the projective Weierstrass models over $A_0$-algebras, assumed chord–tangent (`h𝒢`: for each curve with unit discriminant there is a points evaluation compatible with $\mathcal{G}$) and origin-normalised (`h𝒢O`: the identity section is cut out by a section of the origin chart sending both $x/y$ and $z/y$ to $0$); $\mathcal{T}$ is a `LevelTransport` for $\mathcal{G}$ at $q$, functorial in algebra maps and equivariant for variable changes and preserving the level condition, assumed to be a section transport (`h𝒯`, the two clauses of `LevelTransport.IsSectionTransport` relating the transported sections $P$ and $Q$ to the original ones along the graded maps induced by variable changes, respectively by coefficient maps). `hVC` asserts that for every $A_0$-algebra $T$, projective Weierstrass curve $W$ over $T$ and variable change $C$ there is a graded ring homomorphism $\varphi$ from `projModelGradingCR W` to `projModelGradingCR (C • W)` such that the irrelevant ideal of the target is contained in the image under $\varphi$ of the irrelevant ideal of the source and $\varphi$ satisfies `IsVariableChangeHom W C φ` (it fixes constants and acts on the three coordinates by the formulas of the variable change $C$); `hCO` asserts the analogous existence for a coefficient map $f : T \to_{A_0} T'$, with $\varphi$ satisfying `IsCoefficientHom W f.toRingHom φ` (it applies $f$ to constants and fixes the three coordinates).
--
--   Fifth group (the auxiliary cyclotomic frame). $L_1$ is a field of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of order $q\ell_g$, with $\zeta_1$ a primitive $q$-th root of unity, $\xi_1$ a primitive $q\ell_g$-th root of unity, and $\zeta_1 = \xi_1^{\ell_g}$. $K_1$ is the intermediate field `laurentBaseChange L₁ (xHFunctionField (q ^ 2 * M') H₁)` of `LaurentSeries L₁`. $A_1$ is a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L_1$ such that $q$ lies in its maximal ideal and $\zeta_1$ is in the image of $A_1 \to L_1$; $K_1$ carries an $A_1$-algebra structure compatible with $A_1 \to L_1 \to K_1$. The element $j_1 \in K_1$ is nonzero with underlying Laurent series `coeffEmb L₁ jq`, and $\varpi_1$ generates the maximal ideal of $A_1$. On the two-chart integral model $X_1 :=$ `TwoChartIntegralModel A₁ ↥K₁ j₁` (the pushout of the two spectra of the integral charts `chartAlgFin` and `chartAlgInf`), $z_1$ is a point, $\varpi z_1$ is the germ at $z_1$ of the global section obtained from $\varpi_1$ through `toBase` (`hϖz₁`), and `hz₁` requires $\varpi z_1$ to lie in the maximal ideal of the stalk at $z_1$, so that $z_1$ lies on the special fibre. The point $y_1$ of `XFin A₁ ↥K₁ j₁ = Spec (chartAlgFin A₁ ↥K₁ j₁)`, where `chartAlgFin A₁ ↥K₁ j₁` is the subalgebra of elements of $K_1$ integral over $A_1[j_1]$, maps to $z_1$ under `ιFin` (`hy₁`). Finally `hss₁` requires that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi$ from `chartAlgFin A₁ ↥K₁ j₁` to $\Omega$ with kernel $y_1$, the image $\varphi(j_1)$ of `jChartFin` lies in `ssJSet q Ω`, i.e. every elliptic curve over $\Omega$ with that $j$-invariant has no nonzero point killed by $q$.
--
--   Sixth group (the comparison maps). $\iota : L_1 \to k_0$ is a ring homomorphism with $\iota(\zeta_1) = \xi$; `hιA` says that $\iota$ carries $A_1$ into $A$, and `hιA'` that conversely any $x \in L_1$ with $\iota(x) \in A$ comes from $A_1$, so $A_1$ is the contraction of $A$ along $\iota$. Lastly $c$ is a ring homomorphism from $C_1 :=$ `chartAlgFin A₁ ↥K₁ j₁` to $C' :=$ `chartAlgFin ↥A₀ ↥K j` such that for every $a \in C_1$ the Laurent series over $k_0$ attached to $c(a)$ is [`ModularCurve.coeffMap ι`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L16) applied to the Laurent series over $L_1$ attached to $a$; that is, $c$ acts on $q$-expansions coefficientwise through $\iota$.
--
--   Conclusion. There exists an element $y'$ of the subtype of those ideals $y$ of $C'$ which are maximal and contain the image of $\pi_0$ under `algebraMap ↥A₀ ↥C'`, such that `Ideal.comap c y'.1 = y₁.asIdeal`; in other words, some maximal ideal of the finite $j$-chart $C'$ over $A_0$ contains the uniformiser $\pi_0$ and contracts along $c$ to the prime of $C_1$ corresponding to $y_1$.
--
--   This is the going-up step that transports the supersingular special-fibre point $y_1$ of the integral $j$-chart over the cyclotomic discrete valuation ring $A_1$ to a closed point of the corresponding chart over $A_0 = A \cap k_0$, in the frame where the auxiliary level is the $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$-condition combined with triviality of the diamond action, and where the level subgroup $H_1$ consists of the units of $\mathbb{Z}/q^2M'$ congruent to $1$ modulo $q$ and modulo $\ell_g$. It is used by [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.Diamond.exists_ringHom_chartAlgFin_levelAut_comap_eq_of_isLevelAutAt_of_ringHom_cyclotomic_rigidDataGamma1Pow`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.Diamond.exists_ringHom_chartAlgFin_levelAut_comap_eq_of_isLevelAutAt_of_ringHom_cyclotomic_rigidDataGamma1Pow) in the analysis of the semistable reduction of the modular curve at $q$.
